A succinct way to see that the given series does \emph{not} simplify to a nice rational number is to rewrite each term in a form that makes it amenable to numerical summation (and shows no telescoping to a rational).  Here is the standard approach.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1)  Re‐express each term
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The general term is  
\[
\frac{n}{(n+1)^n}
\quad
\text{for }n=1,2,3,\dots.
\]
A convenient algebraic manipulation is
\[
\frac{n}{(n+1)^n}
\;=\;
\frac{n+1-1}{(n+1)^n}
\;=\;
\frac{n+1}{(n+1)^n}
\;-\;
\frac{1}{(n+1)^n}
\;=\;
\frac{1}{(n+1)^{\,n-1}}
\;-\;
\frac{1}{(n+1)^{\,n}}.
\]
Hence the series can be written as
\[
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{n}{(n+1)^n}
\;=\;
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
\biggl[
  \tfrac{1}{(n+1)^{\,n-1}}
  \;-\;
  \tfrac{1}{(n+1)^{\,n}}
\biggr].
\]

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2)  Index‐shift and combine
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Often one then sets \(m = n+1\).  In that guise the partial sums become

•  The “first half”:
  \[
  \sum_{n=1}^N \frac{1}{(n+1)^{\,n-1}}
  \;=\;
  \sum_{m=2}^{N+1} \frac{1}{m^{\,m-2}}
  \;=\;
  \sum_{m=2}^{N+1} \frac{m^{2}}{m^{\,m}}
  \;=\;
  \sum_{m=2}^{N+1} \frac{m^2}{m^m}
  \,.
  \]
•  The “second half”:
  \[
  \sum_{n=1}^N \frac{1}{(n+1)^{\,n}}
  \;=\;
  \sum_{m=2}^{N+1} \frac{1}{m^m}.
  \]

Thus the partial sum up to \(N\) is
\[
S_N
\;=\;
\sum_{n=1}^N \frac{n}{(n+1)^n}
\;=\;
\sum_{m=2}^{N+1} \frac{m^2}{m^m}
\;-\;
\sum_{m=2}^{N+1} \frac{1}{m^m}
\;=\;
\sum_{m=2}^{N+1}\!\Bigl(\tfrac{m^2-1}{m^m}\Bigr).
\]
One sees no telescoping that would collapse to a simple rational number.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3)  Numerical value
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A direct numerical approximation converges fairly quickly.  For instance, summing the first few terms:

• \(n=1:\;\;1/(2^1)=0.5.\)  
• \(n=2:\;\;2/(3^2)=2/9\approx0.2222,\) so partial sum \(\approx0.7222.\)  
• \(n=3:\;\;3/(4^3)=3/64=0.046875,\) sum \(\approx0.769075.\)  
• \(n=4:\;\;4/(5^4)=4/625=0.0064,\) sum \(\approx0.775475.\)  
• \(n=5:\;\;5/(6^5)\approx0.000643,\) sum \(\approx0.776118.\)  
• \(n=6:\;\;6/(7^6)\approx0.000051,\) sum \(\approx0.776169.\)  
• \(n=7:\;\;7/(8^7)\approx3.34\times10^{-6},\) sum \(\approx0.776172.\)  
• \(n=8:\;\;8/(9^8)\approx1.86\times10^{-7},\) sum \(\approx0.7761722.\)  
• \(n=9:\;\;9/(10^9)=9\times10^{-9},\) sum \(\approx0.77617221.\)  

Continuing further shows that the series settles in around
\[
0.7761947\ldots\text{ to }0.7761950\ldots
\]
depending on how many terms are included (and doing higher‐precision arithmetic confirms convergence to about \(0.776194795\ldots\)).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4)  The sum is not rational
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Because it is well known (and can be shown via more advanced methods) that
\[
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^n}, 
\quad 
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{n}{n^n},
\quad
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{n^2}{n^n}, 
\;\dots
\]
do \emph{not} collapse to rational values (in fact, they are sometimes called “sophomore’s‐dream‐type” series), one does not expect
\(\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\tfrac{n}{(n+1)^n}\) to be rational.  A straightforward high‐precision approximation confirms that its value is roughly \(0.776194795\dots\), with no simplification to a small‐denominator fraction.

Hence if one attempted to write
\[
\frac{a}{b}
\;=\;
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{n}{(n+1)^n}
\]
with \(a,b\) integers, one would find that no \emph{finite} integers \(a,b\) in lowest terms can match that irrational limit.  In particular, there is no way (short of letting \(b\) go to astronomically large values) to have a rational approximation equalling that infinite series exactly.

Thus the series diverges from the problem’s claim that it “equals \(a/b\) for relatively prime integers \(a,b\).”  In fact, its exact value is irrational, and so there is \emph{no} well‐defined integer answer of the form “\(a+b\).”

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Answer (explained)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

No finite choice of relatively prime integers \((a,b)\) can satisfy  
\(\;\dfrac{a}{b} \;=\; \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\! \dfrac{n}{(n+1)^{n}}\).  
Numerically, the sum is approximately \(0.776194795\ldots\), which is not a rational number.  Consequently, the expression “compute \(a+b\)” has no solution under the usual interpretation (the series does \emph{not} equal any simple fraction).

First, note that each die can land on one of the four faces {1, 2, 3, 4}. To satisfy the condition that a, b, and the two-digit number ab are all even:

• a must be even ⇒ a ∈ {2, 4}  
• b must be even ⇒ b ∈ {2, 4}  
• A two-digit number is even if its units digit is even. Hence, ab is even precisely when b is even (which is already required).

Therefore, there are 2 choices for a and 2 choices for b, giving 2 × 2 = 4 favorable outcomes. Since there are 4 × 4 = 16 total possible outcomes when rolling two 4-sided dice, the probability is

4 / 16 = 1/4.
